{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbiyYPxxNr7GzLExYp4H", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A list is mutable and a tuple is immutable. That is the main difference, and the others follow from it.\n\n**Lists** (`[1, 2, 3]`)\n- You can change them after creation: append, remove, reassign items, sort in place.\n- They are typically used for collections of similar items whose size or contents may change.\n- They can't be dictionary keys or set members, because they aren't hashable.\n\n**Tuples** (`(1, 2, 3)`)\n- You can't change them after creation. There is no `append` and no item assignment.\n- They are typically used for fixed groupings of possibly different types, like `(name, age)` or `(x, y)` coordinates.\n- They are hashable if all their elements are, so they can be dictionary keys or set members.\n- They are slightly faster and use a bit less memory.\n- Functions use them to return multiple values, and unpacking works on them: `x, y = point`.\n\n```python\nlst = [1, 2, 3]\nlst[0] = 99          # fine\n\ntup = (1, 2, 3)\ntup[0] = 99          # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n**Caveats**\n- A single-element tuple needs a trailing comma: `(1,)`. Without it, `(1)` is just the integer 1.\n- Tuples are only shallowly immutable. If a tuple holds a list, that list can still be modified: `([1, 2], 3)[0].append(4)` works.\n\nUse a list when the collection will change. Use a tuple when it represents a fixed record or needs to be a dictionary key."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7210, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7210}, "output_tokens": 1, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "con